# Writings / Content

**Write on Substack; this site mirrors it.** Substack is where posts are drafted, published, and commented on. `src/content/posts/` holds a full-text mirror so we own an on-brand reading experience, a searchable archive, and a home that survives Substack. Neither side sets a `canonicalURL`; search engines pick.

Do **not** hand-edit a mirrored post (any file with `substackUrl` in its frontmatter) — the next sync overwrites it. Fix the text on Substack and re-run the sync.

## Syncing

```bash
npm run sync:substack        # add --dry-run to preview
```

`scripts/sync-substack.mjs` pulls both publications' RSS feeds, converts the post HTML to markdown, archives images into `src/assets/writings/<slug>/`, rewrites cross-post links to local `/writings/` URLs, and prettier-formats what it writes. `.github/workflows/sync-substack.yml` runs it daily. Publications, skipped slugs, and per-slug frontmatter overrides live in `scripts/substack-sources.mjs`.

## Frontmatter (required unless noted)

```yaml
---
title: "Plain, specific, human"
description: "One honest sentence; used for SEO + cards."
pubDatetime: 2024-03-12T00:00:00Z   # preserve the ORIGINAL publish date
modDatetime:                         # optional, on real edits
tags: ["essays"]                     # default ["others"]; lowercase
featured: false                      # optional; surfaces on home
draft: false                         # optional; hidden in prod when true
author: "Suhaas"                     # byline; "Suhaas" or "Sahil"
substackUrl:                         # set by the sync; marks a mirrored post
substackComments:                    # set by the sync; count at last run
canonicalURL:                        # unused for mirrored posts
---
```

## Hand-authored posts

Anything written here rather than on Substack (no `substackUrl`) is left alone by the sync. Always **preserve original publish dates** so the archive timeline stays honest.

## Voice

Plain, human, specific, written from the reader's side. No jargon, no startup filler. If unsure of exact wording, leave a clearly-marked `[PLACEHOLDER]` for hand-editing rather than inventing slick copy.

**No em-dashes (—)** in titles, descriptions, or body copy. They read as AI punctuation. Use a comma, colon, or a second sentence instead. (En-dashes for number ranges like `15–20` are fine.)

This applies to copy we write. Mirrored post bodies are reproduced verbatim from Substack, punctuation included.
